Some details for the discussion: 

The ISO API uses an array of pointers, such you can e.g. allocate your ISO channels as 
```c
struct bt_iso_chan left;
struct bt_iso_chan right;

cig_param.cis_channels[0] = &left;
cig_param.cis_channels[1] = &right;

int bt_iso_cig_create(&cig_param, &cig);
```

whereas this takes an array of channels, so that you need to do
```c
struct bt_audio_stream streams[2];
struct bt_audio_stream *left = &streams[0];
struct bt_audio_stream *right = &streams[1];

bt_audio_unicast_group_add_streams(group, streams);
```

In other words, the channels need to be allocated in an array, which is less flexible IMO, especially if you need to allocate multiple streams per remove device. The alternative here would be 

```c
struct bt_audio_stream *streams[2];
struct bt_audio_stream left;
struct bt_audio_stream right;

streams[0] = &left;
streams[1] = &right;

bt_audio_unicast_group_add_streams(group, streams, ARRAY_SIZE(streams));
```
